In this lesson we'll look at what happens when the index in a power isn't a nice round whole number, specifically the cases of negative one, negative two, and one half. First up, the reciprocal power, x to the negative one, which is just one divided by x. Picture a hyperbola, sitting in the first and third quadrants, getting closer and closer to the axes without ever touching them. Next, the reciprocal square, x to the negative two, or one divided by x squared. It still hugs those same axes, but because you're squaring the bottom, the whole curve lives above the x-axis and is always positive. Finally, the square root power, x to the half, which is just the square root of x. This one only makes sense for x values that are zero or positive, starts at the origin, and rises more and more gently as x grows, like the top half of a sideways parabola. Now let's see it in action.